  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 315 AM Saturday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1... One more cloudy and fairly cool day today, with \nrain chances highest from early this morning through early \nafternoon. \n\nCold air damming conditions will persist over much of the area \ntoday. The surface frontal zone is analyzed to our S, across N AL/N \nGA, SC, and far SE NC, with a weak trough just W of the mountains \nfrom E TN up through E OH, and narrow and weak surface high pressure \ncentered just off the Canadian Maritimes extending SW into central \nNC. The resulting light low level flow from the N and NE will ensure \nadvection of lower dewpoints into our area today, keeping low level \nstability elevated. PW will remain high through midday, up to 125-\n150% of normal, as a mid level shortwave trough moves in from the \nWNW. This combo of moisture and forcing for ascent should result in \na blossoming of showers over much of the area, starting early this \nmorning and lasting into early afternoon. (Thunderstorm chances are \nnot zero but look to be low, given expected poor instability, and \nwould likely be restricted to far S and SE areas.) The higher rain \ncoverage should then shift into the Coastal Plain during the mid to \nlate afternoon as the shortwave trough heads to our E and the \nbacking low-mid level flow to northwesterly helps to dry out the \ncolumn from NW to SE. \n\nThe signals for widespread heavy rain are not as profound for today \nas we've seen the last few days, given the more limited convective \ncontribution to lift. But the high PW and deep LCL-0C layer do \nsuggest a continued risk for locally soaking downpours, mainly from \nthe Triangle to the S and E, and a few flood advisories may be \nneeded, particularly in those areas that have seen higher rain \ntotals the last few days. \n\nRegarding temperatures, with a period of high rain chances and \nconsiderable cloud cover in the weakening CAD regime, highs will \nonce again be below normal. Expect highs to range from the mid 70s \nNE to low 80s SW, 8 to 15 degrees below normal. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... By the end of the weekend, expect a return to more \ntypical late July weather, with highs around 90 and mostly diurnally \ndriven convection. Severe weather may be possible Tuesday night.\n\nBy Sunday, the front that has been around central North Carolina \nshould have moved far south to have minimal impact locally, and \nhighs should rebound into the 80s everywhere. By Monday, highs will \nreturn to more typical upper 80s and lower 90s, continuing through \nthe week. Tuesday is likely to be the warmest day with highs a few \ndegrees warmer.\n\nPrecipitation should also return to generally being diurnally-\ndriven. While coverage should be isolated most days, the greatest \ncoverage should occur Tuesday night with a front moving through the \nregion. Early yesterday morning, SPC added areas from New Jersey \ndown to the NC/SC border in an outlook for potential severe \nthunderstorms on Tuesday. This discussion is due before SPC issues \ntheir next update around 5am, but the potential certainly remains \nfor strong to severe thunderstorms. Right now, it appears that the \nlimiting factor could be timing of the frontal passage, which would \nlikely be overnight Tuesday into Wednesday, therefore limiting \npotential instability.",
